INT. ASHGROVE MANOR - GREEN DRAWING ROOM - EVENING

Rain on tall windows. A grandfather clock. A tea service on a low table. A bust of a long-dead general on the mantel.

BARNABY CREWE, 40s, a private inquirer in a rumpled coat, stands at the window with his back to the room. LADY PHILOMENA ASHGROVE, 70s, upright in a wing chair, regards him over the rim of her cup. DR. ANSELM ROTH, 50s, the family physician, sits on the settee with a black bag between his feet.

She sets down her cup. He turns.

LADY PHILOMENA
You've been staring at that glass for ten minutes, Mr. Crewe.

CREWE
It's an interesting pane.

LADY PHILOMENA
It is a pane.

He moves to the mantel and runs his thumb over the bust, then wipes the dust on his handkerchief. She watches with disapproval. Roth shifts and she glances at him.

ROTH
Philomena, if Mr. Crewe has questions, let him ask.

CREWE
Thank you, Doctor.

LADY PHILOMENA
Ask, then.

CREWE
Who last saw Lord Ashgrove alive?

LADY PHILOMENA
I did.

ROTH
(together with her)
I did.

CREWE
Both of you?

They look at each other. He leans forward, she lifts her chin.

LADY PHILOMENA
At dinner.

ROTH
At the stairs, afterward.

CREWE
Which was first?

LADY PHILOMENA
Dinner.

ROTH
He was already tired.

She frowns at him. He studies the bag between his feet. Crewe takes the poker from its stand, turns it over, and replaces it exactly where it was.

CREWE
Doctor, what's in the bag?

ROTH
Medical instruments.

CREWE
And the brown bottle?

Roth's hand drops to the bag. She sets her cup down so softly it does not click. The clock begins to strike the hour, and in the third chime Crewe walks to the settee, kneels, and lifts the clasp.

CREWE (CONT'D)
Foxglove. Prepared.

LADY PHILOMENA
(together with Roth)
It was for his heart.

ROTH
(together with Lady Philomena)
It was for his heart.

They stop. The clock finishes. Crewe stands with the bottle in his hand and looks from one to the other.

CREWE
Strange. You rehearsed the same sentence.
